-- title:
--   Theorem 9.1 — No Free Lunch
-- statement:
--   **Statement (Theorem 9.1, p. 153, PDF p. 175).** Consider any domain $X$ of size
--   $|X| = 2m > 4$, and any algorithm $A$ which outputs a hypothesis $A(S)$ given a sample $S$
--   of size $m$. Then there exists a concept $C : X \to \{0,1\}$ and a distribution $D$ such
--   that: the generalization error of $C$ is zero; and with probability at least $1/10$, the
--   error of the hypothesis generated by $A$ is at least $1/10$.
--
--   This motivates the need to restrict the hypothesis class: without such a restriction, no
--   algorithm can learn *every* concept well, even with an unlimited sample of a fixed finite
--   size.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The sample $S$ is $m$ i.i.d. draws from $D$, labeled by $C$
--   (`Measure.pi`, the product measure of $m$ copies of $D$); "generalization error of $C$ is
--   zero" and "error of $A(S)$" both use the zero-one-loss `GeneralizationErrorZeroOne` against
--   the same data-generating distribution (`x ∼ D`, `y = C(x)`).
-- source:
--   Hazan, Introduction to Online Convex Optimization, 2nd ed., arXiv:1909.05207v3, p. 153, Theorem 9.1 (PDF p. 175)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_OnlineConvexOpt_LearningTheory_GeneralizationError

open MeasureTheory

namespace OnlineConvexOpt.LearningTheory

/-- Theorem 9.1, No Free Lunch (Hazan, *Introduction to Online Convex Optimization*, 2nd ed.,
arXiv:1909.05207v3, p. 153, PDF p. 175). Consider any domain `X` of size `|X| = 2m > 4`, and any
algorithm `A` which outputs a hypothesis `A(S)` given a sample `S` of size `m`. Then there exists
a concept `C : X → {0,1}` and a distribution `D` such that: the generalization error of `C` is
zero; and with probability at least `1/10`, the error of the hypothesis generated by `A` is at
least `1/10`.

The sample `S` is `m` i.i.d. draws `x_1,...,x_m ∼ D`, labeled by `C`; `A` maps such a labeled
sample to a hypothesis `X → Bool`. "Generalization error of `C` is zero" is stated for the same
data-generating distribution (`x ∼ D`, `y = C(x)`) that produced `S`. -/
theorem no_free_lunch
    {X : Type*} [Fintype X] [MeasurableSpace X] [MeasurableSingletonClass X]
    (m : ℕ) (hm : Fintype.card X = 2 * m) (hm4 : 4 < 2 * m)
    (A : (Fin m → X × Bool) → X → Bool) :
    ∃ (C : X → Bool) (D : Measure X), IsProbabilityMeasure D ∧
      GeneralizationErrorZeroOne (D.map fun x => (x, C x)) C = 0 ∧
      (1 / 10 : ℝ) ≤
        (Measure.pi (fun _ : Fin m => D)
          {S : Fin m → X | (1 / 10 : ℝ) ≤
            GeneralizationErrorZeroOne (D.map fun x => (x, C x))
              (A (fun i => (S i, C (S i))))}).toReal := by sorry

end OnlineConvexOpt.LearningTheory
